findings.detail:
Why: bytes are not what costs; execution at load is. The corpus measured platonos (84) shipping gsap+ScrollTrigger+SplitText eagerly: 1.78 s bootup, TBT 358 ms, 'its largest remaining front-end cost' (levers.md 7.6, levers-research.json). TBT is summed between FCP and TTI, so even `requestIdleCallback` after load can land inside the TBT window on a 4x-throttled CPU; a user gesture never happens in Lighthouse, and an IntersectionObserver on a below-fold element never fires without scrolling. MDN: `import()` 'allows loading an ECMAScript module asynchronously and dynamically into a potentially non-module environment' and 'is always available'. WordPress 6.5 introduced `wp_enqueue_script_module()` which prints `<script type="module">` (deferred by spec); `wp_enqueue_script()` supports `'strategy' => 'defer'` since 6.3 for the classic alternative. GSAP's own WordPress guide enqueues gsap as a dependency and waits for `window` `load` — fine for desktop demos, but on this plugin's sites it puts ~50 KB gz of parse+ScrollTrigger.refresh() before TTI.

child theme functions.php:
```php
add_action('wp_enqueue_scripts', function () {
  if (is_admin() || \Elementor\Plugin::$instance->preview->is_preview_mode()) return;
  wp_enqueue_script_module('child-motion-loader', get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/js/motion-loader.js', [], '1.0.0');
});
// loader must survive optimizers: nowprocket (WP Rocket), data-no-optimize (LiteSpeed)
add_filter('wp_script_attributes', fn($a) => ($a['id'] ?? '') === 'child-motion-loader-js-module' ? $a + ['nowprocket' => true, 'data-no-optimize' => '1'] : $a);
```
js/motion-loader.js:
```js
const first = document.querySelector('[data-anim]');
if (first && !document.body.classList.contains('elementor-editor-active')) {
  let started = false;
  const start = () => { if (started) return; started = true; io?.disconnect(); import('/wp-content/themes/CHILD/js/motion.js'); };
  const io = 'IntersectionObserver' in window ? new IntersectionObserver(e => e.some(x => x.isIntersecting) && start(), { rootMargin: '300px 0px' }) : null;
  io?.observe(first);
  for (const ev of ['scroll', 'pointerdown', 'keydown', 'touchstart']) addEventListener(ev, start, { once: true, passive: true });
}
```
js/motion.js (ESM, self-hosted):
```js
import gsap from './vendor/gsap/index.js'; import { ScrollTrigger } from './vendor/gsap/ScrollTrigger.js';
gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);
ScrollTrigger.config({ ignoreMobileResize: true, limitCallbacks: true });
const mm = gsap.matchMedia();
mm.add({ fine: '(pointer: fine) and (min-width: 1025px)', reduce: '(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)' }, (ctx) => {
  const { reduce, fine } = ctx.conditions;
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-anim="fade-up"]').forEach(el => {
    if (el.getBoundingClientRect().top < innerHeight) return; // already on screen: leave static, no flash
    gsap.from(el, { y: fine ? 40 : 24, autoAlpha: 0, duration: reduce ? 0 : 0.8, ease: 'power2.out',
      scrollTrigger: { trigger: el, start: 'top 85%', once: true } });
  });
});
```
Elements carry `data-anim|fade-up` through Pro Advanced > Attributes (v3) or the v4 General tab Attribute section (finding 5). Do not hide targets in CSS (`.reveal{opacity:0}`): if JS never runs (Delay-JS, error, bot) the content stays invisible and an LCP candidate can disappear.

MDN BCD 8.1.6: `animation-timeline`, `scroll()`, `view()`, `animation-range`, `ScrollTimeline`, `ViewTimeline` all Chrome/Edge 115, Safari+iOS 26, Samsung 23, Firefox 'preview' only. Chrome doc: timelines 'integrate with the existing Web Animations API and CSS Animations API ... run off the main thread'; `animation-timeline` is not part of the `animation` shorthand and 'must be declared after the animation shorthand'; set duration `auto`; `view()` tracks the subject in its nearest scroller; ranges `entry`, `exit`, `cover`, `contain`, e.g. `animation-range: entry 25% cover 50%`; ranges use the untransformed box so scaling during scroll does not flicker; named timelines: `view-timeline-name: --x` / `scroll-timeline: --x inline`, `timeline-scope` for non-ancestors. WebKit guide: wrap in `@media not (prefers-reduced-motion)`. Exact pattern (final state is the default, so unsupported browsers see finished layout):
```css
.reveal{opacity:1}
@supports (animation-timeline: view()){
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference){
    .reveal{animation:reveal-in auto linear both;animation-timeline:view();animation-range:entry 0% entry 60%}
    .cards>:nth-child(2){animation-range:entry 10% entry 70%}
    .cards>:nth-child(3){animation-range:entry 20% entry 80%}
  }
}
@keyframes reveal-in{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(24px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}
/* progress bar */
#progress{transform-origin:0 50%;animation:grow auto linear both;animation-timeline:scroll(root)}
@keyframes grow{from{transform:scaleX(0)}to{transform:scaleX(1)}}
/* parallax layer, below the fold only */
.band img{animation:drift auto linear both;animation-timeline:view();animation-range:cover 0% cover 100%}
@keyframes drift{from{transform:translateY(-8%)}to{transform:translateY(8%)}}
```
`both` fill is required so below-fold subjects hold the `from` state before entry; a subject already in view on load is past `entry 100%` and shows the final state, so first-screen elements never animate. Polyfill `scroll-timeline-polyfill` 1.1.0 is 60,892 B raw / 16,753 gz and main-thread: do not ship it.

BCD: `Element.animate` Chrome 36, Edge 79, Firefox 48, Safari 13.1/iOS 13.4. MDN: `el.animate(keyframes, {duration, iterations, fill, easing, delay})` returns an `Animation` with `play()/pause()/reverse()/finish()/cancel()`, `playbackRate`, `finished` promise; `offset` per keyframe. anime docs: `waapi.animate()` is '3KB versus 10KB' for the JS version, prioritise it 'when initial page load time is critical and every KB counts' and 'animating during CPU/network load'; use `animate()` for > 500 targets, SVG/attribute/canvas values, complex timelines. Hardware-accelerated in every major browser: opacity, transform, translate, scale, rotate; in some: clip-path, filter; 'Safari (desktop and mobile) currently won't trigger hardware acceleration if the animation is using a custom linear() easing' (anime's 'out(3)', 'inOut(3)' and any JS easing passed to waapi.animate) -> use `cubic-bezier()` or named eases. Zero-dependency reveal module (first-screen elements are marked done and never animated; hidden state only exists once the module has run, so Delay-JS or no-JS leaves everything visible):
```js
// child theme assets/js/reveal.js, loaded as type="module" (finding 7)
const els=[...document.querySelectorAll('.js-reveal')];
const ok=matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)').matches&&'animate' in Element.prototype&&'IntersectionObserver' in window;
if(!ok){els.forEach(el=>el.classList.add('is-in'));}
else{
  const vh=innerHeight;
  const io=new IntersectionObserver(entries=>{for(const e of entries){if(!e.isIntersecting)continue;io.unobserve(e.target);e.target.classList.add('is-in');
    e.target.animate([{opacity:0,transform:'translateY(24px)'},{opacity:1,transform:'none'}],{duration:600,easing:'cubic-bezier(.2,.7,.2,1)',fill:'backwards',delay:+e.target.dataset.delay||0});}},{rootMargin:'0px 0px -10% 0px'});
  els.forEach(el=>{el.getBoundingClientRect().top<vh?el.classList.add('is-in'):io.observe(el);});
  document.documentElement.classList.add('reveal-ready');
}
```
```css
.reveal-ready .js-reveal:not(.is-in){opacity:0}
```
Stagger: set `data-delay="0|60|120"` on siblings or compute `i*60`. Counter: in the same callback, `requestAnimationFrame` loop over 800 ms writing `el.textContent`. anime equivalent: `import {waapi, stagger} from 'animejs'; waapi.animate(cards,{translate:'0 0',opacity:1,delay:stagger(60),duration:600,ease:'cubic-bezier(.2,.7,.2,1)'})`.

Facts: Dynamic Content for Elementor on voreniahorizon.gr loads `assets/node/animejs/anime.min.js` = anime.js v3.2.2 (17,384 B raw / 7,113 gz) as global `anime`; the v4 UMD also defines a global `anime` object, so loading both overwrites one. levers.md 7.1: LiteSpeed 7.9 skips scripts whose `type` is not `text/javascript` and `data-no-optimize`; WP Rocket needs `nowprocket`; Perfmatters 'Excluded from Delay' and FlyingPress exclusions are keyword lists; Delay-JS that rewrites a module loader leaves the effect dead until the first tap, and Googlebot never interacts (breakage-checklist). design-to-elementor.md: 'No <script> in HTML widgets; scripts live in the child theme'. Pattern (classic enqueue so `script_loader_tag` can add the attributes):
```php
add_action('wp_enqueue_scripts', function () {
  if ( ! is_singular() || ! get_post_meta( get_queried_object_id(), '_ingl_motion', true ) ) return; // per-page opt-in
  wp_enqueue_script( 'ingl-motion', get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/js/motion.min.js', [], '1.0.0', [ 'in_footer' => true, 'strategy' => 'defer' ] );
});
add_filter('script_loader_tag', function ( $tag, $handle ) {
  return 'ingl-motion' === $handle ? str_replace( '<script ', '<script type="module" data-no-optimize="1" nowprocket ', $tag ) : $tag;
}, 10, 2);
```
`motion.min.js` is the esbuild output of finding 4/6 (reveal.js alone, or reveal + `import {waapi, stagger} from 'animejs/waapi'`), written to the theme via Novamira `write-file`. Inside the module: bail out on `matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)')` false; never select `.e-con:first-of-type` children; no `window.anime` export. Add the token `ingl-motion` to Perfmatters 'Excluded from Delay' / FlyingPress exclusions (LiteSpeed skips `type=module` + `data-no-optimize` already).

Chrome doc: cross-document transitions 'rely on the very same building blocks' as same-document ones; trigger is a same-origin navigation (scheme+host+port), both pages must opt in with `@view-transition { navigation: auto; }` (navigation types traverse/push/replace, not address-bar or reload); skipped with `TimeoutError` if the navigation takes > 4 s; customise with `pageswap`/`pagereveal` events (the listener 'must be registered in a classic parser-blocking script in the <head>' or marked `blocking=render`), `navigation.activation` for direction-aware types, `types` in the at-rule, `view-transition-name` set just-in-time and removed after `viewTransition.finished/ready`. BCD 8.1.6: `@view-transition` Chrome/Edge 126, Safari/iOS 18.2, Samsung 28, Firefox none; same-document `startViewTransition` Chrome 111, Safari 18, Firefox 144. Plugin (wordpress.org/plugins/view-transitions): 'implements support for cross-document view transitions in WordPress ... by default using a fade effect', version 1.2.1, 10,000+ active installs, requires WP 6.9, tested 7.1.3, settings under Settings > Reading, default duration 400 ms, 'Respect prefers-reduced-motion settings' (1.2.0), theme can declare its own configuration. Minimal manual version for Hello/child theme:
```css
@view-transition { navigation: auto; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { ::view-transition-group(*), ::view-transition-old(*), ::view-transition-new(*) { animation: none !important; } }
::view-transition-old(root), ::view-transition-new(root) { animation-duration: .35s; }
.site-header { view-transition-name: header; } /* stays put while content fades */
```
Shared hero morph (room card -> room page) needs unique names: set `style.viewTransitionName='room-hero'` on the clicked card image in `pageswap` and on the detail hero in `pagereveal`, as in the Chrome 'Profiles' example. Elementor Pro 'Page Transitions' (Site Settings > Page Transitions) are an overlay effect plus an optional Preloader that 'operate[s] when visitors arrive at your site' (help page) - a first-arrival preloader covers the LCP by design; its JS cost was not measured.

MDN: 'CSS transitions are by default not triggered on an element's initial style update, or when its display type changes from none to another value. To enable first-style transitions, @starting-style rules are needed'; 'especially useful when creating entry and exit transitions for elements displayed in the top layer (such as popovers and modal <dialog>s), elements that are changing to and from display: none, and elements when first added to or removed from the DOM'; two forms (standalone block or nested); same specificity as the original rule so it must come after it; only relevant to transitions, not `@keyframes`. BCD: `@starting-style` Chrome/Edge 117, Firefox 129, Safari/iOS 17.5, Samsung 24. Pattern for a custom off-canvas panel or any Elementor element toggled by a class:
```css
.panel{opacity:1;translate:0 0;transition:opacity .3s,translate .3s cubic-bezier(.2,.7,.2,1)}
.panel.is-open{ @starting-style { opacity:0; translate:0 16px; } }
.panel:not(.is-open){opacity:0;translate:0 16px;pointer-events:none}
```
For `display:none` -> shown and `<dialog>`/`[popover]`, add `display .3s allow-discrete` (and `overlay .3s allow-discrete` for top layer) to the transition list as in the MDN popover example (`[popover]:popover-open{opacity:1;transform:scaleX(1);@starting-style{opacity:0;transform:scaleX(0)}}`). Elementor's own Off-Canvas/popup widgets toggle their own classes with JS; the entry transition can still be CSS by targeting the widget's open-state class found in DevTools instead of adding a JS animation library.

Compositor rules from finding 11. Patterns:
```css
.card{transition:transform .25s cubic-bezier(.2,.7,.2,1),filter .25s}
.card img{transition:transform .5s cubic-bezier(.2,.7,.2,1)}
@media (hover:hover) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference){
  .card:hover{transform:translateY(-4px);filter:drop-shadow(0 16px 32px rgb(0 0 0/.16))}
  .card:hover img{transform:scale(1.04)}
  .btn{position:relative;overflow:hidden} .btn::after{content:'';position:absolute;inset:0;background:rgb(255 255 255/.18);translate:-101% 0;transition:translate .35s} .btn:hover::after{translate:0 0}
  .link{background:linear-gradient(currentColor,currentColor) 0 100%/0 1px no-repeat;transition:background-size .3s} .link:hover{background-size:100% 1px}
}
.card:active{transform:scale(.98)} /* touch feedback, 1 frame */
```
(`background-size` on the underline is paint-only on a 1 px strip - acceptable; or use a `::after` with `scaleX`.) Elementor 4.x: Global Classes carry hover/focus/active states so the same transitions are set once per class (design-to-elementor.md references the V4 states model; the exact editor UI was not opened in this research). Legacy `hover-animations` (`elementor-animation-grow` etc.) are not in `frontend.min.css` 4.3.5 (0 matches), i.e. they come from an extra stylesheet when used - prefer the explicit transitions above. `@media (hover:hover)` avoids sticky hover states on touch and keeps mobile CSS inert.
